Project: Personal Context Engine (built on MemoryAgent)

1. Vision — read this first, every phase should serve this

Most AI products bundle two different things together: "the model" (the
reasoning engine) and "the memory" (what it knows about you). Every AI
company wants those bundled, because the memory is what locks you into
their product.

This project separates them. The memory of the user lives in a system
the user controls, independent of any single AI provider. The AI model
becomes swappable — Qwen today, Gemini tomorrow, a local model next year
— while the memory persists and travels with the user regardless of
which model answers them.

This is a prototype, not a commercial product yet. Do not add
monetization, multi-tenant user management, or production-grade security
hardening. Optimize for "does the mechanism actually work and can I
demo it," not "is this ready for other people's data."

2. Current state (do not regress any of this)

The project already has a working:


FastAPI backend (app/main.py) exposing /chat, /memories/{user_id},
/health, /maintenance/decay
SQLite-backed persistent memory store (app/memory.py) with
relevance + confidence + recency scoring on recall, and confidence
decay + hard-delete forgetting over time
An LLM client that already supports swapping providers via an
LLM_PROVIDER env var (Qwen, OpenAI, Gemini currently) — this file may
be named app/llm_client.py or app/qwen_client.py depending on which
version is in the repo; check before assuming
A simple web UI (static/index.html, static/style.css,
static/app.js) with:

A name-based "login" (no real auth) that maps a name to a user_id,
stored in browser localStorage
If the same name/user_id is used again, prior memories are recalled
automatically — this is the working cross-session proof and must
keep working through every phase below





Before starting Phase 1, run the existing acceptance test: start the
server, confirm the UI loads, confirm typing a name and chatting works,
confirm a second "login" with the same name recalls prior facts. If this
baseline doesn't work, fix it before adding anything new.

3. Non-goals (explicitly out of scope for this project, all phases)


No real user authentication/password system — name-based localStorage
identification is intentional and sufficient for a prototype.
No multi-tenant production security hardening (rate limiting, input
sanitization beyond basic validation, etc.) unless a specific phase
below says otherwise.
No mobile app. Desktop browser only.
No payment/subscription logic of any kind.
No migrating away from SQLite unless Phase 4 is explicitly reached and
a decision is made to do so — SQLite is the correct choice for a
prototype and should not be "upgraded" prematurely.


4. Phased plan

Phase 1 — OpenRouter as the universal model gateway

Goal: prove the "swappable brain" idea concretely by routing through
OpenRouter instead of maintaining separate integrations per provider.
OpenRouter exposes one OpenAI-compatible endpoint that can reach many
different underlying models (including free-tier models), so this
replaces the need to juggle multiple provider API keys.

Tasks:


Add openrouter as a provider option in the existing LLM client
provider config (base_url: https://openrouter.ai/api/v1, api key
env var: OPENROUTER_API_KEY).
Add a LLM_MODEL env var override (should already partially exist) so
the specific model string (e.g. a :free suffixed model on
OpenRouter) can be changed without touching code.
Add a small GET /providers debug endpoint that returns which
provider + model is currently active, so it's easy to confirm which
brain is currently plugged in without checking env vars manually.
In the UI, add a small, unobtrusive text label (e.g. in a corner or
settings area) showing the current model/provider, pulled from the
new /providers endpoint. This is what makes the "swappable brain"
idea visible and demoable, not just true under the hood.


Acceptance: switching LLM_PROVIDER/LLM_MODEL env vars and restarting
the server changes which model answers, with zero code changes, and the
UI reflects the change. Memory recall behavior is identical regardless
of which model is active — the memories themselves are provider-agnostic
and must not change based on which model wrote them.

Phase 2 — Memory ownership and transparency

Goal: make "the user owns their memory" concretely true and visible,
not just an architectural claim.

Tasks:


Add a GET /memories/{user_id}/export endpoint that returns all of a
user's memories as a single downloadable JSON file.
Add a "View my memory" screen/panel in the UI (reachable from the chat
screen) that lists every stored memory for the current user in plain
language, with:

The memory content
When it was created / last reinforced
A delete button per memory (calls the existing
DELETE /memories/{mem_id} endpoint)
A "download my data" button (calls the new export endpoint)



This screen is the "you can see and control what it knows about you"
proof — it should be simple, readable, not a raw JSON dump in the main
UI (the export button can give raw JSON; the on-screen list should be
human-readable).


Acceptance: a user can see every fact stored about them in plain
English, delete any individual fact, and export everything as a file,
entirely through the UI, without needing to query the API directly.

Phase 3 — Cross-application memory (the core differentiator)

Goal: prove memory can be reused outside of this specific chat UI —
the actual "not locked into one app" claim.

Tasks (pick the simpler sub-option first; only attempt the harder one if
time allows):

3a. Manual bridge (simpler, do this first):


Add a "Copy memory context" button in the UI that copies the current
user's recalled/full memory set to the clipboard as a short paragraph
(e.g. "Known facts about this user: user is vegetarian, allergic to
peanuts, prefers concise answers.") formatted to be pasted directly
into the system prompt or first message of any other AI chat tool
(ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini web UI, etc).
Add a companion "Learn from pasted conversation" input: a text box
where the user pastes a conversation they had with a different AI
tool, and the backend runs the existing memory-extraction step
(MEMORY: ... pattern) against that pasted text to pull out new facts
and store them, exactly as if it had been said in this app's chat.


This alone proves the concept end-to-end: memory extracted from one AI
tool, reused in another, with a human doing the copy/paste bridging.

3b. Browser extension (harder, only if time allows after 3a works):


A minimal browser extension (Manifest V3) that, when active on a
known AI chat site (e.g. chat.openai.com, gemini.google.com,
claude.ai), reads the current draft text in the message box before
the user hits send, calls this backend's /chat-adjacent memory
recall (a new lightweight GET /memories/{user_id}/context endpoint
that just returns the relevant memory text, no LLM call) and prepends
it to the outgoing message automatically.
This is a genuinely separate, larger sub-project. Treat it as its own
milestone with its own testing, not something to rush inside a single
session. Do not attempt this until 3a is fully working and demoed.


Acceptance for 3a: a user can have a conversation in this app, copy a
memory summary, paste it into an unrelated AI tool's chat, and get a
response from that other tool that reflects the same known facts — this
is the demo that proves the core idea.

Phase 4 — Only if the project continues past prototype stage

Not to be started without an explicit decision to do so:


Migrate storage from SQLite to a self-hosted or small managed
Postgres instance, for multi-device access.
Add per-user encryption of memory content at rest.
Replace keyword-overlap memory scoring with embedding-based semantic
similarity (using any provider's embedding endpoint).
Per-category memory decay rates (permanent facts like allergies vs.
fast-decaying state like "currently reading X") instead of one global
half-life.


5. Constraints that apply to every phase


Never break the existing name-based login → memory recall flow. This
is the working core and every new feature is additive to it, not a
replacement.
Keep using plain HTML/CSS/JS for the frontend — no framework, no
build step, consistent with the existing UI.
Keep the memory store as SQLite through Phase 3. Do not "upgrade" the
database prematurely — it is correctly scoped for a prototype with a
single user testing it.
Every new endpoint should be tested manually (curl or the /docs
Swagger UI) before being wired into the frontend, so backend and
frontend issues are debugged separately, not simultaneously.
After each phase, re-run the full existing acceptance test (login,
chat, re-login with same name, confirm recall) before starting the
next phase.


6. Suggested order of work in a single Antigravity session


Confirm baseline still works (see Section 2).
Phase 1 in full (OpenRouter integration + visible model indicator).
Phase 2 in full (memory viewer + export + delete in UI).
Phase 3a only (copy/paste bridge) — stop here unless there's
significant remaining time.
Do not start Phase 3b or Phase 4 without a fresh, explicit go-ahead —
they are meaningfully bigger scopes than everything above combined.